069,0.0,-0.0011175584513694048,-1.050330638885498,-0.2731815278530121,-0.09501104801893234,-0.04298940673470497,-0.0011560192797333002,-0.21563903987407684,-0.04765187203884125,-2.062299427052494e-05,-0.004484358709305525,0.0,-0.002014394849538803,-0.07744872570037842,-5.960446742392378e-06,-9.536738616588991e-07,-0.0014462736435234547,-2.1319990158081055,-0.20782147347927094,-0.0006858142442069948,-1.9311717551317997e-05,-1.4066597032069694e-05,-1.311301275563892e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,64,4779,400,65,4779,400,66,4779,323,400,67,3,387,6785,1931,5109,1741,429,400,18,21,64,488,220,19,65,488,220,19,66,488,220,18,67,284,220,17,20,2418,220,7379,279,7192,897,315,198,78045,64,1124,15136,1124,26888,90,65,92,1124,15136,1124,26888,58,18,15370,66,92,1124,15136,1124,26888,58,19,15370,67,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d$ be positive real numbers such that $36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25.$  Find the maximum value of\n\\[a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2de17349d2d1c86b044b03c314cdb17fa7aaf2bf8b169f36bc351ecf136830e7"},"seconds":40.07467079162598,"seed":342747749177922974,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the maximum value of \\(a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}\\) given the constraint \\(36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25\\), we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or the AM-GM inequality. Here, we will use the AM-GM inequality for simplicity.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression we want to maximize:\n\\[ a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d} = a \\times b^{1/2} \\times c^{1/3} \\times d^{1/4}. \\]\n\nTo apply the AM-GM inequality, we need to find a way to balance the terms. Consider the expression:\n\\[ 36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25. \\]\n\nWe can rewrite the product \\(a \\times b^{1/2} \\times c^{1/3} \\times d^{1/4}\\) in a form that allows us to use the AM-GM inequality. We need to find a way to make the exponents of the terms in the AM-GM inequality equal. Let's consider the following substitutions:\n\\[ 36a, \\quad 2b, \\quad 2b, \\quad 3c, \\quad 3c, \\quad \\frac{3d}{2}, \\quad \\frac{3d}{2}. \\]\n\nWe have 7 terms in total, and we want to use the AM-GM inequality on these terms. The AM-GM inequality states that the arithmetic mean of a set of non-negative numbers is at least the geometric mean of the same set. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{36a + 2b + 2b + 3c + 3c + \\frac{3d}{2} + \\frac{3d}{2}}{7} \\geq \\sqrt[7]{36a \\cdot 2b \\cdot 2b \\cdot 3c \\cdot 3c \\cdot \\frac{3d}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3d}{2}}. \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{36a + 4b + 6c + 3d}{7} = \\frac{25}{7}. \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{25}{7} \\geq \\sqrt[7]{36a \\cdot 2b \\cdot 2b \\cdot 3c \\cdot 3c \\cdot \\frac{3d}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3d}{2}}. \\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{25}{7} \\geq \\sqrt[7]{36 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\cdot a \\cdot b^2 \\cdot c^2 \\cdot d^2} = \\sqrt[7]{36 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 9 \\cdot a \\cdot b^2 \\cdot c^2 \\cdot d^2} = \\sqrt[7]{2916 \\cdot a \\cdot b^2 \\cdot c^2 \\cdot d^2}. \\]\n\nFor equality to hold in the AM-GM inequality, we need:\n\\[ 36a = 2b = 2b = 3c = 3c = \\frac{3d}{2} = \\frac{3d}{2}. \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ 36a = 2b = 3c = \\frac{3d}{2}. \\]\n\nLet \\(36a = 2b = 3c = \\frac{3d}{2} = k\\). Then we have:\n\\[ a = \\frac{k}{36}, \\quad b = \\frac{k}{2}, \\quad c = \\frac{k}{3}, \\quad d = \\frac{2k}{3}. \\]\n\nSubstituting these into the constraint \\(36a + 4b + 4c + 3d = 25\\), we get:\n\\[ 36 \\left( \\frac{k}{36} \\right) + 4 \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) + 4 \\left( \\frac{k}{3} \\right) + 3 \\left( \\frac{2k}{3} \\right) = 25. \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ k + 2k + \\frac{4k}{3} + 2k = 25. \\]\n\\[ k + 2k + \\frac{4k}{3} + 2k = \\frac{3k + 6k + 4k + 6k}{3} = \\frac{19k}{3} = 25. \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{75}{19}. \\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(k = \\frac{75}{19}\\) back into the expressions for \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\):\n\\[ a = \\frac{k}{36} = \\frac{75}{19 \\cdot 36} = \\frac{75}{684} = \\frac{25}{228}, \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{75}{38} = \\frac{75}{38}, \\]\n\\[ c = \\frac{k}{3} = \\frac{75}{57} = \\frac{25}{19}, \\]\n\\[ d = \\frac{2k}{3} = \\frac{150}{57} = \\frac{50}{19}. \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the product \\(a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d}\\):\n\\[ a \\times \\sqrt{b} \\times \\sqrt[3]{c} \\times \\sqrt[4]{d} = \\frac{25}{228} \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{75}{38}} \\times \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{25}{19}} \\times \\sqrt[4]{\\frac{50}{19}}. \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{25}{228} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{75}}{\\sqrt{38}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{25}}{\\sqrt[3]{19}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{50}}{\\sqrt[4]{19}} = \\frac{25}{228} \\times \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{38}} \\times \\frac{5^{1/3}}{19^{1/3}} \\times \\frac{2^{1/2} \\cdot 5^{1/4}}{19^{1/4}}. \\]\n\nAfter simplifying, we find that the maximum value is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:40"}
